{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession","id":1023108,"title":"Time Travel Through Possession","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-27T21:44:00-08:00","id":1023108,"title":"Time Travel Through Possession","content":"So I've been watching the Netflix show Travelers. They use a form of possession and time travel to the moment of a persons death to become them. Well and prevent the death obviously. Which brings me to the idea of an effect that would work similarly though not to prevent the hosts death as that act alone could cause serious paradox. <br /> <br /> Do you think this form of time travel could be a means of reducing the paradox multiplier for traveling backwards in time? Especially if the person possessed was of no consequence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-27T23:08:51-08:00","id":1023134,"title":"","content":"Everyone is of consequence, even if we do not know their names. By preventing a death, you create a situation where that person consumes resources that would have been consumed by other entities, you create a situation where that person has children than would not otherwise have existed, etc. Genius and monsters are born who would have not been born, saints and killers are born who would have not been born, etc. Save a person who would have died two hundred fifty, you will have a thousand different people. Save a person who would have died five hundred years ago, you will have a million different people. Save a person who would have died seven hundred fifty years ago, you will have a billion different people. Eventually, you will replace one of your own ancestors and erase yourself from existence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T06:14:10-08:00","id":1023223,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023134#post1023134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Everyone is of consequence, even if we do not know their names. By preventing a death, you create a situation where that person consumes resources that would have been consumed by other entities, you create a situation where that person has children than would not otherwise have existed, etc. Genius and monsters are born who would have not been born, saints and killers are born who would have not been born, etc. Save a person who would have died two hundred fifty, you will have a thousand different people. Save a person who would have died five hundred years ago, you will have a million different people. Save a person who would have died seven hundred fifty years ago, you will have a billion different people. Eventually, you will replace one of your own ancestors and erase yourself from existence.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this kind of aspect would only come up if that's the focus of your game. In the most realistic sense, the butterfly effect of time travel is extremely powerful, assuming the setting is one that allows changes to the past via time travel at all.<br /> <br /> That said, if time travel is the theme of the game or something focused on, it can be pretty easy to handwave this sort of thing. Because if you don't then having time travel at all is kind of pointless. There is no way to calculate the number of changes and what influence you would have on history overall beyond blind guessing. Killing a butterfly could prevent WWII sort of thing removes much player action to it.<br /> <br /> But in general, it seems a safe practice than just jumping back entirely. It'd probably be of great benefit to learn up on who you intend to replace or character differences might out you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12552-monalfie","name":"Monalfie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12552_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T07:33:33-08:00","id":1023264,"title":"","content":"Research would be key, and I was not suggesting to possess someone and prevent their death. That's just the premise of the show. <br /> <br /> I also agree that the changes to events would be just as paradoxical as any other form of time travel manipulation. <br /> <br /> My question was do you think that this manner of time travel would reduce the paradox multiplier for doing so? By using possession you would not be placing you physical presence in the past. In effect, other than the actions you change, your very presence should not cause paradox since you are present as one who belongs there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T07:54:14-08:00","id":1023274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tuch</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023264#post1023264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Research would be key, and I was not suggesting to possess someone and prevent their death. That's just the premise of the show.<br /> <br /> I also agree that the changes to events would be just as paradoxical as any other form of time travel manipulation.<br /> <br /> My question was do you think that this manner of time travel would reduce the paradox multiplier for doing so? By using possession you would not be placing you physical presence in the past. In effect, other than the actions you change, your very presence should not cause paradox since you are present as one who belongs there.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is pretty much like Quantum Leap.<br /> <br /> What you'd be doing is sending your mind back in time and into another person's body.<br /> This could arguably be considered Coincidental, though it would still garner a load of paradox simply because you're going back in time.<br /> If you were to enter the mind of a sleeper, however, it would be Vulgar with Witnesses.<br /> <br /> Arguably, if the &quot;back in time&quot; thing has a duration on it, say like a few hours, you would only get the paradox after you return to you body/real time, along with all the butterfly effect stuff causing all kinds of weird effects.<br /> <br /> But yeah, Time Travel should never be without consequences, even if the consequences are as simple as Chrononautical Technocrats from the future chasing after you for screwing around with time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T09:16:55-08:00","id":1023306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023134#post1023134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Everyone is of consequence, even if we do not know their names. By preventing a death, you create a situation where that person consumes resources that would have been consumed by other entities, you create a situation where that person has children than would not otherwise have existed, etc. Genius and monsters are born who would have not been born, saints and killers are born who would have not been born, etc. Save a person who would have died two hundred fifty, you will have a thousand different people. Save a person who would have died five hundred years ago, you will have a million different people. Save a person who would have died seven hundred fifty years ago, you will have a billion different people. Eventually, you will replace one of your own ancestors and erase yourself from existence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Vastly overrated. Genetics evens out pretty quickly. Sure, if one of your great-great-great-grandparents wasn't around or was a different person, it would have some effect, but, unless there was something really significant (like causing a war, spreading a plague or the like), that's changing 1 out of 32 people who contributed to &quot;you&quot;. Without a significant change, events would be taking their previous course... those 31 other people would be the same, marry the same people, have the same children, end up having the same descendants so much like you that differences are mostly so insignificant that the &quot;new&quot; you would be almost exactly.<br /> <br /> If you want to dedicate your time to messing with a modern person's history, you could make a difference. Or if you find someway to change the events of the past significantly (like creating a hurricane to wipe out a few towns, enlisting the ancestors to go fight in a war they never fought in before and making sure they never come home, etc.), you might break all that up. But that's begging for enough Paradox to melt your bones and have Wrinkle show up to claim your floppy body.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T12:00:21-08:00","id":1023369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023306#post1023306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Vastly overrated. Genetics evens out pretty quickly. Sure, if one of your great-great-great-grandparents wasn't around or was a different person, it would have some effect, but, unless there was something really significant (like causing a war, spreading a plague or the like), that's changing 1 out of 32 people who contributed to &amp;quot;you&amp;quot;. Without a significant change, events would be taking their previous course... those 31 other people would be the same, marry the same people, have the same children, end up having the same descendants so much like you that differences are mostly so insignificant that the &amp;quot;new&amp;quot; you would be almost exactly.<br /> <br /> If you want to dedicate your time to messing with a modern person's history, you could make a difference. Or if you find someway to change the events of the past significantly (like creating a hurricane to wipe out a few towns, enlisting the ancestors to go fight in a war they never fought in before and making sure they never come home, etc.), you might break all that up. But that's begging for enough Paradox to melt your bones and have Wrinkle show up to claim your floppy body.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are vastly underestimating how dynamic genetics can be, and the sheer amount of random chance there is involved with geneology.<br /> <br /> If you so much as change the day your parents had sex, who's to say the same sperm will meet the same egg? Instead of you being born, it would be potential sibling.<br /> Heck, just changing the exact second sex begins can effect which sperm reaches the egg first.<br /> <br /> This can translate into huge differences in what the child turns out to be, such as genetic defects, innate abilities, or something as big as what gender the child is.<br /> <br /> All of this will have a knock on effect in history leading to further changes and thus more factors which would potentially change even more things.<br /> <br /> The children would not be the same, and thus the geneology may be completely different.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T12:00:40-08:00","id":1023370,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023306#post1023306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Vastly overrated. Genetics evens out pretty quickly. Sure, if one of your great-great-great-grandparents wasn't around or was a different person, it would have some effect, but, unless there was something really significant (like causing a war, spreading a plague or the like), that's changing 1 out of 32 people who contributed to &quot;you&quot;. Without a significant change, events would be taking their previous course... those 31 other people would be the same, marry the same people, have the same children, end up having the same descendants so much like you that differences are mostly so insignificant that the &quot;new&quot; you would be almost exactly.<br /> <br /> If you want to dedicate your time to messing with a modern person's history, you could make a difference. Or if you find someway to change the events of the past significantly (like creating a hurricane to wipe out a few towns, enlisting the ancestors to go fight in a war they never fought in before and making sure they never come home, etc.), you might break all that up. But that's begging for enough Paradox to melt your bones and have Wrinkle show up to claim your floppy body.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even just a simple scenario can cause someone to meaningful cease to exist as a person. Let's say you're an Etherite and your big enemy is Bob, a complete douchebag who is always stealing your ideas. So you build your Time Travel Device and go back in time to screw him over by changing the past of his grandpa, Joe. <br /> <br /> Joe was born in a rural town and married Beth. They lived their all their lives and had a son they called Steve who married a young girl there named Angelina. Those two picked up stakes and moved out to California where they had a child named Bob who ends up Awakening and become your rival. <br /> <br /> You go back in time and manage to convince Joe to marry a different girl, Tiffany. Tiffany doesn't live living in rural, po-dunk nowhere and convinces Joe to move with her to New York. The two have a daughter there by the name of Mary. Mary ends up marrying a fellow named Tom who was in New York on business but lives down in Florida, and she ends up moving down there with him. The two of them have a daughter named Cheryl. Cheryl might also Awaken (but she might not) even if she did, that doesn't necessarily mean she'll be your rival - she might not even end up joining the Sons of Ether.<br /> <br /> Really, what happens in an RPG with time travel just just depends on how deep down the rabbit hole the ST is willing to go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T12:34:02-08:00","id":1023379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023369#post1023369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You are vastly underestimating how dynamic genetics can be, and the sheer amount of random chance there is involved with geneology.<br /> <br /> If you so much as change the day your parents had sex, who's to say the same sperm will meet the same egg? Instead of you being born, it would be potential sibling.<br /> Heck, just changing the exact second sex begins can effect which sperm reaches the egg first.<br /> <br /> This can translate into huge differences in what the child turns out to be, such as genetic defects, innate abilities, or something as big as what gender the child is.<br /> <br /> All of this will have a knock on effect in history leading to further changes and thus more factors which would potentially change even more things.<br /> <br /> The children would not be the same, and thus the geneology may be completely different.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really, as I teach genetics in the context of physical anthropology. I'm pretty dead on on how flexible the gene pool of a human population can be. In fact, that one great great great grandparent could be a Neanderthal and it wouldn't make much of a difference to the genome of the 7th generation descendant. And Neanderthals are arguably not even the same species as modern humans. <br /> <br /> Persistence of populations without some influencing event was pretty much de jure until the later part of the 20th Century. So, unless you are messing with the last 3 or so generations, the likelihood is that pretty much the same people or people very similar to them would make their contributions to the descendant as the set of events would probably run that same as well. The mage would have to intervene substantially and repetitively to get the kind of effects from the &quot;butterfly effect&quot;. The world is a little more robust than it appears with the perspective of decades over years and centuries over decades. <br /> <br /> It won't matter if John becomes Jane, unless John was someone really important. And even then, if Jane's not eligible, the person who takes John's place is going to be a lot like John was. So there might be some minor changes, but, after 3 generations or so, even those differences aren't going to mean all that much.<br /> <br /> Take the historical events that matter and try to figure out how to change them. It usually won't work. How do you stop the Black Death or the Plague of Justinian? Even when a person is of seminal importance, that doesn't mean they aren't ultimately fungible. Will killing Temujin stop the Mongols from conquering or was the world ripe for Gengis Kahn and someone like him would have arisen even if it wasn't that exact kid? They don't have to be... that's the way to really derail history. It's not making sure Bill doesn't have any kids. There are thousands of Bills out there to pick up the slack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T13:13:55-08:00","id":1023397,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023369#post1023369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You are vastly underestimating how dynamic genetics can be, and the sheer amount of random chance there is involved with geneology.<br /> <br /> If you so much as change the day your parents had sex, who's to say the same sperm will meet the same egg? Instead of you being born, it would be potential sibling.<br /> Heck, just changing the exact second sex begins can effect which sperm reaches the egg first.<br /> <br /> This can translate into huge differences in what the child turns out to be, such as genetic defects, innate abilities, or something as big as what gender the child is.<br /> <br /> All of this will have a knock on effect in history leading to further changes and thus more factors which would potentially change even more things.<br /> <br /> The children would not be the same, and thus the geneology may be completely different.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I see your point if I'm the ST running a time travel story, or even allowing it for the session, I don't think I'm going to be this anal. It's a game, and as we really don't have a frame of perspective on what exaclty messing with some areas of the past really would do I think I'm inclined to go with Ajax's interpretation. Right or wrong.<br /> <br /> Additionally, every change made in the timestream will attempt to correct itself thus lending more creedence to this idea. If time is changed to drastically you get an alternate timeline or Mr. Wrinkle (or both).<br /> <br /> I also dont see possession as vulgar with witnesses. Especially if the mind of the host isn't aware of the situation. Hell in many times of hisotry, including many beliefs today, possession fully excepted. Though admittedly not by the guy standing next to you.<br /> <br /> Either way, none of this gets to my actual question as to if this form of time travel could reduce the paradox multiplier. The effects of time travel and manipulation of the time stream will be as paradoxical as ever. The damage once can or cannot produce are likewise up to the ST and the type of story that is being ran. I just wondered if whatever type of story was being told if the possession of someone in the time being traveled to could reduce the paradox gained of if it was felt that the travel itself was where the paradox came from and not the actual presence of the traveler.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T13:25:09-08:00","id":1023401,"title":"","content":"Well, there's a ritual in <i>Blood Sacrifice: The Thaumaturgy Companion</i> called <b>Turn Back the Skies</b> that works like this. I'd say if vampiric ritual magic can accomplish it, suitably advanced true magick ought to be able to without automatically vaporizing the mage. Of course, the storyteller would have to read up on it to see if the system described is in tune with the way time travel functions in his or her chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T13:37:10-08:00","id":1023407,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023379#post1023379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not really, as I teach genetics in the context of physical anthropology. I'm pretty dead on on how flexible the gene pool of a human population can be. In fact, that one great great great grandparent could be a Neanderthal and it wouldn't make much of a difference to the genome of the 7th generation descendant. And Neanderthals are arguably not even the same species as modern humans.<br /> <br /> Persistence of populations without some influencing event was pretty much de jure until the later part of the 20th Century. So, unless you are messing with the last 3 or so generations, the likelihood is that pretty much the same people or people very similar to them would make their contributions to the descendant as the set of events would probably run that same as well. The mage would have to intervene substantially and repetitively to get the kind of effects from the &quot;butterfly effect&quot;. The world is a little more robust than it appears with the perspective of decades over years and centuries over decades.<br /> <br /> It won't matter if John becomes Jane, unless John was someone really important. And even then, if Jane's not eligible, the person who takes John's place is going to be a lot like John was. So there might be some minor changes, but, after 3 generations or so, even those differences aren't going to mean all that much.<br /> <br /> Take the historical events that matter and try to figure out how to change them. It usually won't work. How do you stop the Black Death or the Plague of Justinian? Even when a person is of seminal importance, that doesn't mean they aren't ultimately fungible. Will killing Temujin stop the Mongols from conquering or was the world ripe for Gengis Kahn and someone like him would have arisen even if it wasn't that exact kid? They don't have to be... that's the way to really derail history. It's not making sure Bill doesn't have any kids. There are thousands of Bills out there to pick up the slack.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really, every human being is unique. There are 1,400 CNVs (copy number variants) that differ between humans and even if the average CNV only possesses two common variations, there will be over 2 * 10 ^ 421 different combinations of just CNVs that can be expressed among human beings, and that is just a fraction of the human genome. That means that there are more potential combinations for just CNVs in humans than there will ever likely be humans in existence, which means that every human (except possibly identical twins) will be a unique individual who will potentially have a unique influence on the future of humanity. <br /> <br /> Of course, the divergence will be greater the future you go back in time for your possession, but I would suggest that going back 2,000 years would cause dramatic changes in the course of history, even if the individual was 'unimportant'. How important would a herder in the steppes of Mongolia seem in 16 AD? Well, they are probably one of the ancestors of Genghis Khan, so even taking them over for a day might change the entire course of human history. When you return to 2016, the Japanese may have been the people who colonized the Americas and they (and their Native American allies) are dealing with issues of illegal immigration from Europe. Of course, this is why Paradox realms exist, to show Mages why it is a bad idea to go back into the past.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T13:50:14-08:00","id":1023413,"title":"","content":"This sounds weird even as I type it, but watch this terrible B-movie! <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrill_Seekers_(film)\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrill_Seekers_(film)</a><br /> <br /> The basic idea is that someone in the future invents time travel, and so naturally a company begins selling pre-set travel back to huge disasters in the world's history so future-humans can rubberneck at them. A current-time hero figures this out and prevents lots of these disasters. This changes the future timeline, so the time-travelers have more and more trouble reaching home base on their time-videophones and are eventually told they can never return, as the inventor of the time-travel mechanism was killed in a car crash by one of the random guys saved from the most recent disaster. Oh no! The time travelers decide to fake one of the disasters that was prevented (artificially setting bombs at a stadium that was originally destroyed by a fire from the restaurant), trying to undo the changes to the timestream.<br /> <br /> It's honestly not a good movie but it's fun and presents a view on time travel paradox. Certainly it's easy for a GM to say that Wrinkle shows up so give me your character sheet, but it's not <i>interesting</i>.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, it depends on your view of where paradox comes from.<ul><li>Human experience is the important part of the world/Consensus, so the number of changes to human lives and the degree of those changes determines the magnitude of the paradox.<ul><li>Here, sending 800 HIT Marks back to 1970 to murder one lonely guy in his Montana cabin, who would never have left that house and nobody cared about, causes very little paradox.</li> </ul></li> <li>The physical world in which we exist is what matters, so the degree of change to the actual world, human or otherwise, determines the magnitude of the paradox.<ul><li>Devising a brilliant machine to take all of our greenhouse gas emissions and send them back to 10,000 BC would cause a lot of paradox in this view.</li> </ul></li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7356,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","user":"haldir2012","date":". 12-28-2016, 04:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T14:15:46-08:00","id":1023421,"title":"","content":"I have always allowed limited time travel in my games. The limited time travel was always vulgar (there was no way on Heaven or Earth that it could be considered coincidental because it cannot be done through mundane methods at all). In addition, I gave characters one point of Paradox for every day that they traveled back in time, meaning that characters would likely destroy themselves if they traveled back in time more than a week. I required a minimum of five successes per day that they were traveling into the past. <br /> <br /> I justified the limited time travel as a very powerful for a precognition, as the characters were actually using a vulgar form of divination to perceive every permutation of events that would occur but, because of potential bias, they were incapable of remembering that they were attempting to predict the future until they decided to stop the divination. In effect, they created a save point in the past, perceived the future and, when they decided that they had perceived enough, reloaded from the save point. I think that I called the rote 'Memories of the Future', and I required Correspondence 4, Mind 4, and Time 4 (Correspondence 4 to allow the character to reload to the save point, Mind 4 to suppress their memory of the save point to prevent biasing the prediction, and Time 4 for the required 'time loop' to create the save point).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T07:14:48-08:00","id":1023743,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023407#post1023407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not really, every human being is unique. There are 1,400 CNVs (copy number variants) that differ between humans and even if the average CNV only possesses two common variations, there will be over 2 * 10 ^ 421 different combinations of just CNVs that can be expressed among human beings, and that is just a fraction of the human genome. That means that there are more potential combinations for just CNVs in humans than there will ever likely be humans in existence, which means that every human (except possibly identical twins) will be a unique individual who will potentially have a unique influence on the future of humanity.<br /> <br /> Of course, the divergence will be greater the future you go back in time for your possession, but I would suggest that going back 2,000 years would cause dramatic changes in the course of history, even if the individual was 'unimportant'. How important would a herder in the steppes of Mongolia seem in 16 AD? Well, they are probably one of the ancestors of Genghis Khan, so even taking them over for a day might change the entire course of human history. When you return to 2016, the Japanese may have been the people who colonized the Americas and they (and their Native American allies) are dealing with issues of illegal immigration from Europe. Of course, this is why Paradox realms exist, to show Mages why it is a bad idea to go back into the past.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope. You're looking at a very tiny tiny eensey-weensey itty-bitty little granular bit and extrapolating it out to apply to large scale issues. This isn't about individuals and their individual genomes. It's about gene pools. They aren't the same thing. The particularized incidents of semi-random mating are effectively inconsequential. This is is basic evolutionary biology and population genetics. Subtracting one person doesn't matter diddly in the long run. You need to remove LOTS of people. Even expanding rapidly (from say, hundreds of thousands to millions to billions in a temporal heartbeat) doesn't affect the situation much due to the low incident of relevant mutations. <br /> <br /> Overall, all humans aren't very genetically distinct, due to a population bottleneck, probably about 50,000 years ago (at least evolutionary geneticists pick that date, archaeologists see some validity in it, but there are some huge outstanding questions). That means that even dividing up and then reconnecting has happened so fast, genetically speaking, reduces any real effect of contribution from different populations. <br /> <br /> In the end, that means that people are all so closely related to each other that within a very small number of generations, it doesn't really matter all that much who an individuals actual ancestors are, they are effectively as related to each other as they are to any other human. That's why I picked &quot;great-great-great-grandparents&quot; as that is actually one generation one further back than that occurs. And generations are FAST among humans. Arguably 20 years each, which means you can cover that many generations in roughly a century. <br /> <br /> That means the chances of not getting the biological individual you know go down really fast as you go back in time. Meaning the chance/amount of Paradox for killing people's ancestors goes down just as fast. Kill one or both of the parents to negate the child. Yup. Eat the Paradox. Kill grandparents? Depends on how many, but you're still in dangerous territory. Kill great-grandparents.... the chances are getting smaller pretty fast, unless you start offing most of them, but, even then, if the root population is still there (say, you didn't kill the whole village), you're in safer territory. At great-great-grandparents, you are going to have to work really hard to make a difference.<br /> <br /> Obviously, that's all about biology and that's only a partial component to affecting the course of human events, but, arguably, culture and history are bound up in similar ways. (There really isn't any discipline that deals with this topic from this angle. Seems like a sorta theoretical approach to psychohistory a la Aasimov). Possibly/probably they are even MORE resilient due to the omni-directional nature of memes (original meaning, not the current internet version) as opposed to the unidirectional arrow of genetic descent. And, depending on the granular nature of the event that the mage is trying to change. Some things are going to be REALLY HARD to affect because they aren't tied to a single point. In fact, that's most of them. Some are and might be much more easily changed with greater effects. Which is a bad thing to the person of the mage in question... Less change would seem to equal less Paradox, more change = more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T08:38:42-08:00","id":1023778,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023743#post1023743\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nope. You're looking at a very tiny tiny eensey-weensey itty-bitty little granular bit and extrapolating it out to apply to large scale issues. This isn't about individuals and their individual genomes. It's about gene pools. They aren't the same thing. The particularized incidents of semi-random mating are effectively inconsequential. This is is basic evolutionary biology and population genetics. Subtracting one person doesn't matter diddly in the long run. You need to remove LOTS of people. Even expanding rapidly (from say, hundreds of thousands to millions to billions in a temporal heartbeat) doesn't affect the situation much due to the low incident of relevant mutations. <br /> <br /> Overall, all humans aren't very genetically distinct, due to a population bottleneck, probably about 50,000 years ago (at least evolutionary geneticists pick that date, archaeologists see some validity in it, but there are some huge outstanding questions). That means that even dividing up and then reconnecting has happened so fast, genetically speaking, reduces any real effect of contribution from different populations. <br /> <br /> In the end, that means that people are all so closely related to each other that within a very small number of generations, it doesn't really matter all that much who an individuals actual ancestors are, they are effectively as related to each other as they are to any other human. That's why I picked &amp;quot;great-great-great-grandparents&amp;quot; as that is actually one generation one further back than that occurs. And generations are FAST among humans. Arguably 20 years each, which means you can cover that many generations in roughly a century. <br /> <br /> That means the chances of not getting the biological individual you know go down really fast as you go back in time. Meaning the chance/amount of Paradox for killing people's ancestors goes down just as fast. Kill one or both of the parents to negate the child. Yup. Eat the Paradox. Kill grandparents? Depends on how many, but you're still in dangerous territory. Kill great-grandparents.... the chances are getting smaller pretty fast, unless you start offing most of them, but, even then, if the root population is still there (say, you didn't kill the whole village), you're in safer territory. At great-great-grandparents, you are going to have to work really hard to make a difference.<br /> <br /> Obviously, that's all about biology and that's only a partial component to affecting the course of human events, but, arguably, culture and history are bound up in similar ways. (There really isn't any discipline that deals with this topic from this angle. Seems like a sorta theoretical approach to psychohistory a la Aasimov). Possibly/probably they are even MORE resilient due to the omni-directional nature of memes (original meaning, not the current internet version) as opposed to the unidirectional arrow of genetic descent. And, depending on the granular nature of the event that the mage is trying to change. Some things are going to be REALLY HARD to affect because they aren't tied to a single point. In fact, that's most of them. Some are and might be much more easily changed with greater effects. Which is a bad thing to the person of the mage in question... Less change would seem to equal less Paradox, more change = more.</div> </div> </div> </div>So are you implying that scientifically the type of children born to parents are not as numerous as the number of egg and sperm that exists? In otherwords though there are seemingly millions of combinations available there are actually way less?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T08:57:17-08:00","id":1023790,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023743#post1023743\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nope. You're looking at a very tiny tiny eensey-weensey itty-bitty little granular bit and extrapolating it out to apply to large scale issues. This isn't about individuals and their individual genomes. It's about gene pools. They aren't the same thing. The particularized incidents of semi-random mating are effectively inconsequential. This is is basic evolutionary biology and population genetics. Subtracting one person doesn't matter diddly in the long run. You need to remove LOTS of people. Even expanding rapidly (from say, hundreds of thousands to millions to billions in a temporal heartbeat) doesn't affect the situation much due to the low incident of relevant mutations. <br /> <br /> Overall, all humans aren't very genetically distinct, due to a population bottleneck, probably about 50,000 years ago (at least evolutionary geneticists pick that date, archaeologists see some validity in it, but there are some huge outstanding questions). That means that even dividing up and then reconnecting has happened so fast, genetically speaking, reduces any real effect of contribution from different populations. <br /> <br /> In the end, that means that people are all so closely related to each other that within a very small number of generations, it doesn't really matter all that much who an individuals actual ancestors are, they are effectively as related to each other as they are to any other human. That's why I picked &amp;quot;great-great-great-grandparents&amp;quot; as that is actually one generation one further back than that occurs. And generations are FAST among humans. Arguably 20 years each, which means you can cover that many generations in roughly a century. <br /> <br /> That means the chances of not getting the biological individual you know go down really fast as you go back in time. Meaning the chance/amount of Paradox for killing people's ancestors goes down just as fast. Kill one or both of the parents to negate the child. Yup. Eat the Paradox. Kill grandparents? Depends on how many, but you're still in dangerous territory. Kill great-grandparents.... the chances are getting smaller pretty fast, unless you start offing most of them, but, even then, if the root population is still there (say, you didn't kill the whole village), you're in safer territory. At great-great-grandparents, you are going to have to work really hard to make a difference.<br /> <br /> Obviously, that's all about biology and that's only a partial component to affecting the course of human events, but, arguably, culture and history are bound up in similar ways. (There really isn't any discipline that deals with this topic from this angle. Seems like a sorta theoretical approach to psychohistory a la Aasimov). Possibly/probably they are even MORE resilient due to the omni-directional nature of memes (original meaning, not the current internet version) as opposed to the unidirectional arrow of genetic descent. And, depending on the granular nature of the event that the mage is trying to change. Some things are going to be REALLY HARD to affect because they aren't tied to a single point. In fact, that's most of them. Some are and might be much more easily changed with greater effects. Which is a bad thing to the person of the mage in question... Less change would seem to equal less Paradox, more change = more.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're assuming Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium within a large closed population. In a small population, say a cut off village, genes can become extinct or ubiquitous in as few as four generations. Whereas when a small number of new genes enter the pool from an outside source, the equilibrium can be shifted resulting in ubiquity or extinction depending on chance. And then there's the prospect of selective pressures. Environmental effects which can be so circumstantial, such as famines, disease, natural shifts in weather, all of which can be effected by the chaotic shifts of a single change. War, for example can lead to all kinds of selective pressures, and entire battles can be won or lost for want of a single nail.<br /> <br /> Anyway, moving away from academics, the idea that Time alone is not enough to shift history could be argued as fitting in line with the spheres. It may be that, while Time is needed to move around in time, perhaps Entropy is required to actually change anything. Entorpy 4 for anything genetic, and Entropy 5 for anything sociopolitical.<br /> <br /> Certainly it would be a question of paradigm though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T09:24:17-08:00","id":1023811,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1023108-time-travel-through-possession?p=1023790#post1023790\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're assuming Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium within a large closed population. In a small population, say a cut off village, genes can become extinct or ubiquitous in as few as four generations. Whereas when a small number of new genes enter the pool from an outside source, the equilibrium can be shifted resulting in ubiquity or extinction depending on chance. And then there's the prospect of selective pressures. Environmental effects which can be so circumstantial, such as famines, disease, natural shifts in weather, all of which can be effected by the chaotic shifts of a single change. War, for example can lead to all kinds of selective pressures, and entire battles can be won or lost for want of a single nail.<br /> <br /> Anyway, moving away from academics, the idea that Time alone is not enough to shift history could be argued as fitting in line with the spheres. It may be that, while Time is needed to move around in time, perhaps Entropy is required to actually change anything. Entorpy 4 for anything genetic, and Entropy 5 for anything sociopolitical.<br /> <br /> Certainly it would be a question of paradigm though.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're missing the population bottleneck aspect. Humans are very genetically similar to each other. VERY similar. If the total gene pool of humanity 100,000 years ago were a standard deck of 52 cards, we ended up loosing like 30 cards worth of genes. Add in a few from crossbreeding with close relatives (Neanderthals, Denisovans, whatever else those other genes are) and one or two that sum up all the relevant genetic mutation, and you are talking about a small variety of genetic &quot;cards&quot; out there. It doesn't matter how big the deck is now, it's just lots and lots of copies of that smaller number of cards. <br /> <br /> So, yes, even in a small group, those effects come into play. In addition, the modern concept of restricted travel in the past is pretty much false. Villages weren't isolated. They were part of numerous long distance down the line exchange networks which included enough genetic material to keep those from being cut-off as you are depicting them. (More modern travel, since the Age of Exploration, isn't really all that much greater, it's just that it allows for more distant points to have contact with each other. Think of a tapestry where people have to travel across the surface until long distance ships come along, Those ships allow one to fold the fabric and allow different parts of the tapestry to touch). Remember, during the Lower Paleolithic, there was enough genetic &quot;sloshing&quot; occurring that Homo erectus didn't differentiate enough to cause speciation across the entire Eastern Hemisphere. And modern humans were even better at keeping in touch with the folks next door and across significant gaps (like between the then present Greater Indonesian Peninsula extending out from SE Asia and the large combo New Guinea/Australia). <br /> <br /> I agree with your Sphere choices. Though, perhaps, it would be more like using Time like Correspondence to &quot;reach/go back&quot; and Entropy to find the right places to effect any real change. And those HUGE associated Paradox costs and high numbers of successes needed are indicative of much of the above. The &quot;Continuum of History&quot; resists significant change on a large scale because individual events or people just don't typically don't matter when compared to the warp and weft of, well, everything that was. <br /> <br /> However, as a codicil... Those events, places and people that DO matter are probably going to be heavily guarded in some way by time savvy mages that have an interest in keeping history the way it is. After all, there are some points in time and some people (individual or group) that really matter in both the magical and mundane history, and messing with those would cause significant changes. The kind that can wipe out pretty much everything the meddlers came from and make them walking Paradoxes. (Say &quot;Hello&quot; to the nice man in the white suit that desperately needs some dry cleaning....)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T06:55:54-08:00","id":1024286,"title":"","content":"CSB time!<br /> <br /> this story revolves around role playing and how one person can be the cause of others very existance. many lots years ago i ran a D&amp;D game for some friends in and around an anime club i atended. i was also a player in a WW game on the other side of town with a completly different group of people. one guy at the D&amp;Dgame i shall call Smokey wasnt happy. i remember watching him smoke ciggarettes like he was mad at them. i though he might like to join us at the WW game to get a change of scenery. he loved it, and as time progressed he ended up roomates to the storyteller of the WWgame. there, he shared couchspace with the woman who has born two of his children- for who he has gone to war and collage to support. the simple invitation to play in our werewolf game altered the course of his life and resulted in two entirely new human beings entering the world. small things matter in the course of our lives and our interactions with others is a huge stewing mass of life altering choices we may never realize are important until years later. <br /> the least you should expect from screwing with time is double paradox.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>there are no bad ideas, just ideas that have not been refined enough","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1608-iamthesweeper","name":"IAmTheSweeper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1608_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Jo